`ca::fs::descriptor`: an owned POSIX file descriptor whose flags are typed options (the
`ca::fs` options of layer 3), combined and checked at compile time.

```c++
#include "ca/descriptor.hpp"
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/file.hpp"
#include "ca/fs.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

auto main(void) -> int {
	constexpr auto rw = ca::fs::readable | ca::fs::writable | ca::fs::create | ca::fs::truncate | ca::fs::cloexec;
	auto d = ca::fs::descriptor::open("descriptor_example.tmp", rw, 0600U);
	if (!d || !d->write_all(ca::sv::str{"hello, world"}))
		return 1;

	auto at = d->seek(7);
	auto rest = d->read_all();
	if (!at || !rest || rest->size() != 5U || (*rest)[0] != 'w')
		return 2;
	char head[5];
	auto got = d->read_at(head, 0U);
	if (!got || ca::sv::str{head, *got} != ca::sv::str{"hello"})
		return 3;

	*d |= ca::fs::nonblock;
	auto on = d->test(ca::fs::nonblock | ca::fs::cloexec);
	if (!on || !*on)
		return 4;
	*d &= ~ca::fs::nonblock;

	auto status = d->stat();
	if (!status || status->size() != 12U)
		return 5;
	auto again = ca::fs::descriptor::open("descriptor_example.tmp", ca::fs::writable | ca::fs::create | ca::fs::exclusive);
	if (again || again.error() != ca::errc::exists)
		return 6;
	return rest->cleanup() && d->cleanup() && ca::sys::unlink("descriptor_example.tmp") ? 0 : 7;
}
```

### Options

`open(path, options, mode)` (`make` under its generic name, what `ca::make` calls) takes an
option set: readable when no access option is given, `mode`
applying with `create`. Contradictions fail to compile (`exclusive` without `create`, `truncate`
on a read-only file), and an option the platform lacks (`clofork` on Linux) fails with
`errc::not_supported` before anything happens.

After opening, `d |= options` turns options on, `d &= ~options` off, and `test(options)` checks
them. Each option knows its domain, so only the fcntl calls the domains need are made: one
F_SETFD for `cloexec`, one F_SETFL for `nonblock` and `append`. `set` and `clear` are the fallible
forms, all or nothing (an earlier domain rolls back when a later one fails), and report an
unsupported option as `errc::not_supported`; the operators trap on failure and refuse an
unsupported option at compile time. Open-time options (`create`, `truncate`) cannot change
afterwards, and `d &= options` without the `~` is deleted, since it would clear every other flag:
both are compile errors.

### Ownership

A descriptor is one int and relocates with a memcpy. `cleanup()` closes it (the descriptor is gone
even when close fails, and a closed one succeeds); the destructor closes silently as a failsafe;
assigning over an open one traps. `adopt(fd)` takes ownership of a raw descriptor, `release()`
gives it up, `native()` reads it without giving it up, and `duplicate(options)` opens a second
descriptor on the same open file, sharing offset and status flags.

The I/O methods mirror `ca::sys`: `read`, `read_all`, `write`, `write_all`, `read_at`, `write_at`,
`seek`, `sync`, `sync_data`, `resize`, `allocate`, `chmod`, `chown`, `stat`. For buffered
I/O, wrap it in an `io::reader` or `io::writer` (borrowed) or a `buffered_reader` /
`buffered_writer` (adopted).
